WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.290
- English is the most widely
spoken language on the planet.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.790
Research estimates that there are upwards

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.790
of 1.5 billion English speakers worldwide.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.800
That's billion with a B,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.550
more than 18% of the
entire global population.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.460
But how did English become the
most widely spoken language

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.450
in the world,

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.540
and why are there so many
different kinds of Englishes?

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.120
Well, the first kind
of answers the second.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.370
There are so many
different world Englishes

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.950
because there are just
so many English speakers,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.000
with diverse cultural

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.060
and societal influences
impacting their language use.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.030
I'm speaking American English
because I was born, raised.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.920
and educated in the United States,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.420
but there are over 160 different
Englishes around the world.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.340
And as more people learn English,

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.620
more varieties of English are developing.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.360
It's even the language of the air

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.480
and of the sea, Aviation
English and Seaspeak.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.111
In fact, English language
education is considered so crucial

00:00:55.111 --> 00:00:58.770
that 138 countries require
English to be taught

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.380
as part of the mandatory core curriculum.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.183
That's 70% of the countries in the world,

00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:07.680
and another 40 offered as
an optional course of study.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.323
So in total, at least 91%
of countries have some sort

00:01:11.323 --> 00:01:13.770
of formal English language curriculum.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.290
But the real question is why English,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.240
and why not some other language?

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.200
Let's jump in.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.580
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.687
and this is "Otherwords".

00:01:28.966 --> 00:01:31.133
- [Speaker] "Otherwords".

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.892
- English wasn't always that popular.

00:01:34.892 --> 00:01:38.493
In fact, French used to be the
go-to language for diplomacy

00:01:38.493 --> 00:01:41.310
and business when
communicating between entities

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.500
that didn't share the same language,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.420
otherwise known as a lingua franca.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.290
Now, lingua franca literally
means language of the Franks,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.123
which at the time

00:01:50.123 --> 00:01:53.142
of its creation, really referred
to all Western Europeans,

00:01:53.142 --> 00:01:56.490
not just the people who
live in what is now France.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.330
It derives its same

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.628
from the original Lingua
Franca, capital L, capital F,

00:02:00.628 --> 00:02:03.630
which developed in the Mediterranean
basin through borrowing

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.820
from several languages
and was used for trade

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.450
and diplomacy in that area from
the 11th to 18th centuries.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.460
Capitalized Lingua Franca is actually

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.160
a pigeon language, but that's
a topic for another day.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.270
Back to French.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.500
After the Norman Conquest

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.230
in the 11th century, William
the Conquerer installed

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.630
French-speaking nobles to rule England,

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.370
who quickly replaced English
with French in all domains

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.720
of power, from the courts
of law to the church.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.700
Although the peasants still spoke English,

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.380
the aristocracy maintained French

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.890
as their preferred language
for the next few hundred years.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.240
And by the 14th century,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.180
French was the most widely
spoken language in Europe.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.450
By the 17th century, France
was the leading land power

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.790
in Europe, and French was well established

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.500
as the language of prestige,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.370
replacing Latin

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.220
as the language of diplomacy
in international relations.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.070
So why aren't we all
speaking French right now?

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.096
Well, one, we aren't all diplomats,

00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:56.643
but even if we were,

00:02:56.643 --> 00:03:00.005
there was a huge global power
shift in the the 20th century,

00:03:00.005 --> 00:03:00.838
(canon fires)

00:03:00.838 --> 00:03:02.310
the World Wars.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.870
English was already predominant

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:04.770
in the technology

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.860
and innovation sectors thanks
to the Industrial Revolution.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.080
But after the first World War,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.170
the negotiated treaties were
written not just in French,

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.050
but also English, which was a big deal,

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.892
giving English space
in the diplomacy scene.

00:03:18.892 --> 00:03:20.730
After World War II,

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.940
France was frankly no
longer a dominant power.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.430
English, the defacto language

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.740
of the new major economic powerhouse,

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.090
the United States, became the language

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.580
of the economy, and money talks.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.640
Put simply, English is
the most popular language

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.741
in the world because of money and power.

00:03:38.741 --> 00:03:41.250
And American English
is particularly popular

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.860
because of pop culture
influences imported all

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.880
over the world, including
TV, music, food, and cinema.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.980
So that answers our first question,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.690
but what about the second one?

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.750
Why are there so many
different varieties of English?

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.680
Well, first, there is
no set standard English.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.660
Unlike French, whose
standard on everything

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.150
from usage and vocabulary to grammar,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.410
and even the dictionary,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.840
is enforced by the Academie Francaise,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.360
English does not have
any such authoritative

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.820
governing body determining correctness,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:12.862
which means we get a lot

00:04:12.862 --> 00:04:16.440
of variation between Englishes
spoken all around the world,

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.900
and they're all proper
and authentic Englishes.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.120
Beyond that, it's really the same reason

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.700
why anyone talks differently
from anyone else.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.610
There are cultural, social,
historical, political,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.560
and geographical factors that contribute

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.940
to the way we all speak.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.470
Within the United States,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.840
we see English as based
on cultural heritage,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.480
like African-American
English, Chicano English,

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.710
and Cajun English,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:41.104
or on a region like Pittsburghese
or Appalachian English.

00:04:41.104 --> 00:04:43.440
And if you're from England,
you know the difference

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.570
between Yorkshire and Lancaster English,

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.400
even though the counties are only two

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.660
and a half hours apart.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.200
Many global Englishes are
influenced by local languages.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.900
For example, Singapore
English or Singlish,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.100
which is based on British English

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.990
because of that Colonial history,

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.130
includes elements such

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.110
as discourse particles, and loan words

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.840
from other local languages,
including Malay, Hokkien,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.680
and Tamil.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.540
The same is true with Nigerian English,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.340
where there weren't
words in British English

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.530
that expressed certain cultural concepts

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.430
that were then borrowed

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.140
from Native languages of the region.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.910
Linguists have attempted to organize

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.920
the various Englishes into a model.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.652
The most famous is Braj
Kachru's three-circle paradigm

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:22.950
where countries are labeled
inner circle, outer circle,

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.660
and expanding circle.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.460
The inner circle contains the countries

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.710
whose primary language is English.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.560
The outer circle contains
countries where English

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.840
is used as an official
language or a second language,

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.570
typically countries that were
colonized by the inner circle.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.360
And the expanding circle is
where English is widely used

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.160
but learned as a foreign language.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.110
In other words, everywhere else.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.746
This lends a hierarchical vibe,

00:05:44.746 --> 00:05:46.890
but if you've been
following along, you know

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.530
that no one language is
better than any other.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.010
There are multiple scenarios
about what could happen next.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.681
Maybe all of our Englishes will converge

00:05:54.681 --> 00:05:57.270
because we're all so
much more interconnected,

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.590
or oppositely,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.038
in a Tower of Babel moment,
they'll become so different

00:06:01.038 --> 00:06:03.627
as to be mutually unintelligible.

00:06:03.627 --> 00:06:04.860
Unlikely.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:05.910
More likely,

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.820
if English doesn't remain
the most widely spoken,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.700
it's because another economic
powerhouse sways the tide

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.040
of language popularity
in a different direction.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.730
But for now, English is the
foremost world language.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.620
In an increasingly globalized world,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.510
the most important and exciting part

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.550
about all these different Englishes

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.380
is the fact that if you speak English,

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:28.230
you can communicate with
literally a billion more people

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:29.520
than you otherwise could.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.530
And if you can speak multiple languages,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.330
well, then you're ahead of the game.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.276
Replacing Latin as a language of diplomacy

00:06:35.276 --> 00:06:39.274
and international relations.

00:06:39.274 --> 00:06:42.990
Now, lingua franca,
literal, franca, franca.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.323
English wasn't always that popular.

00:06:46.323 --> 00:06:47.763
Back to French.

